course: dictionary
lesson: 2033
title: Confront: face it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To confront something is to face it, often when it is hard or scary. «When he turned and confronted it, the thing had vanished.» He turned to face the thing, and then it was gone. Try swapping in faced: «When he turned and faced it, the thing had vanished» still makes sense, so confront means face.
explain: You can confront a person, usually to talk about a problem, as in "I will confront my friend about the stolen pencils." You can also confront a fear, which means dealing with it instead of running away from it.
explain: Safety comes first. You may need to confront a problem, but never confront a dangerous person or animal. Get away and tell an adult straight away.
fact: Confront is a verb: confront, confronts, confronted, confronting.
fact: «Confronted» means turned to face.
fact: To confront a problem means to deal with it instead of avoiding it.
fact: Useful phrases: confront someone about something, confront a fear, be confronted with a problem.
fact: Never confront a dangerous person or animal; get away and tell an adult.
exercise: «When he turned and confronted it, the thing had vanished.» What did he do to the thing? | He hid his eyes | He lost it | *He turned to face it | He ran away from it
exercise: Mia wants to confront her friend about the stolen pencils. What is she planning to do? | Forget about the problem | Hide the pencils | Run away home | *Talk to her friend about the problem
exercise: Which phrase means a problem is right in front of you and you must face it? | *Be confronted with a problem | Be asleep in a problem | Be lost in a problem | Be far from a problem
test: Ben decided to confront his fear of the dark. What did he decide to do? | Avoid the dark forever | Sleep with the lights on in another room | Hide under his bed | *Face his fear instead of running from it
test: Which is the safest thing to do if you meet a dangerous dog? | Walk up and confront it | *Get away from it and tell an adult | Shout at it to make it go away | Feed it a biscuit
exam: Which sentence uses confront correctly, meaning to face or deal with something? | She confronted the cake and ate it | *The teacher asked us to confront the problem together | The cake confronted in the oven | The sun confronted the sea
